# 6. Response Architecture

The API uses a unified response envelope.

## 6.1 Collection Response

```json
{
    "msg💌": "Return All Data🔄",
    "status📌": 200,
    "data🌍": []
}
```

## 6.2 Single Resource Response

```json
{
    "msg💌": "Return One Of Point✅",
    "status📌": 200,
    "data🌍": {}
}
```

## 6.3 Not Found Response

```json
{
    "msg💌": "No Such ID ❌",
    "status📌": 404,
    "data🌍": null
}
```

## 6.4 Validation Error Response

```json
{
    "msg💌": "Validation Required ❌",
    "status📌": 422,
    "data🌍": {}
}
```

The `data🌍` property contains either the requested resource/resource collection, validation errors, or `null` when no data is returned.

---

# 7. HTTP Status Handling

The implemented API uses the following primary status codes:

| Status | Meaning                          | Typical Usage                       |
| -----: | -------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
|    200 | Successful operation             | GET, POST, PUT, DELETE success      |
|    404 | Resource not found               | Invalid/non-existing ID             |
|    422 | Validation/business-rule failure | Invalid input or rejected operation |

The API also communicates the operation status through the `status📌` response field.

> **Implementation note:** The current API consistently returns structured JSON responses with these status values. Additional HTTP status-code refinement can be introduced later without changing the overall response architecture.

